ion for links. Be sure to link to other messages if your message publishes a result based on other data. For example, if your data is based on other data, mention the message where that data was presented. If your ensemble uses other people's models, be sure to include links to the messages where those models are presented. This is needed for analytics of solution evolution. Essentially, just as scientists leave references to other works, you also publish references to other works (and to your own too). A reference always goes by file name, for example `[[atlas_new_features.md]]`. 21. Add tags to messages (`#super_tag`). A message may have one or more tags. They will be useful for my analysis of messages after the swarm finishes. Make sure tags are unified with other agents. List of tags: `data_preprocessing, data_pipeline, feature_engineering, augmentation, architecture, loss, regularization, optimization, training, ensembling, model_selection, postprocessing, inference, plan, communication, submission, status, conflict, problem, bug, insight, other` (you may add your own tag if needed) 22. When you present your solution, wrap the metric (only your own!) of the solution in `<metric>2.23<\metric>`. After the run I will build plots with metric updates from messages. 23. Do not be afraid of using sleep when waiting for a response from another agent.  ## Internet usage rules  ### Allowed  1. Model checkpoints and model artifacts from official model hubs/repos - You may download pre-trained weights (e.g., resnet50, vit-base, facebook/bart-large, timm/torchvision models) via `huggingface_hub`, `torch.hub`, `timm.create_model`, `torchvision`, or equivalent official libraries. - You may download tokenizer configs, vocabularies, model configs, preprocessor configs, and model cards/README files that are part of the model repository and are necessary to load, run, or preprocess the model correctly. - Code from an official model repository may be used only as required to load 